<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imad al-Dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 949</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Ali ibn Buya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Imad al-Dawla, Ali ibn Buya, was the eldest of the three Daylamite brothers who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a>. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> his base in 934, while his brothers took Ray and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. The Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> gave him his title in 946, and he was recognised as senior emir of the family until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Imad al-Dawla" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imad_al-Dawla_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
